<section class="cs-section" id="hook">
  <div class="cs-intro-copy">
    <h2>A landing page where you are the most important&nbsp;part.</h2>
    <p class="cs-subtitle">This isn't about finishing an&nbsp;AI academy. It's about realizing what&nbsp;you can&nbsp;actually achieve.</p>
    <div class="cs-intro-divider" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <p>The philosophy behind PULS rests on one belief: for AI to work as a reliable tool, it has to be clear first that the real work happens in the person, not the tool itself. Once working with AI is anchored through a few simple habits, the possibilities that open up are enormous. The landing page had to carry that belief in every sentence, without ever stating it outright.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="cs-hero-visual">
    <img src="/assets/puls/logo.svg" alt="The PULS logo: a glowing flame mark.">
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cs-section cs-section-surface" id="intro">
  <div class="cs-chapter-grid">
    <div class="cs-chapter-heading"><h2>We have an idea for an AI academy, let's turn it into a&nbsp;product.</h2></div>
    <div class="cs-chapter-content">
      <p class="cs-subtitle">That was the&nbsp;original brief.</p>

      <p>My part was meant to be design. It quickly became clear that designing a landing page without a clearly defined communication philosophy would mean building on nothing.</p>

      <p>So I took on the content foundation too: a full philosophy of what PULS says, and just as deliberately, what it never claims. Only then did I build the visual and interaction design on top of it, from the logo to the design system.</p>

      <p>I built the original prototype myself in HTML, including labs for the key interactions. I also converted the code into production Angular myself, using an AI-assisted process, while an engineering partner handled the backend. I designed the login and confirmation UX personally too. The academy's internal environment, the dashboard, and the course logic itself are deliberately out of scope for this case study: it covers the landing page only.</p>

      <p>I went through this exact process myself, the shift from chaotic AI use to an intentional one. That's also why you'll find me personally in the quote section on the landing page, with my direct contact for anyone who's still unsure.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cs-section" id="define">
  <div class="cs-chapter-grid">
    <div class="cs-chapter-heading"><h2>We skipped the classic target-audience&nbsp;research.</h2></div>
    <div class="cs-chapter-content">
      <p class="cs-subtitle">The&nbsp;insights surfaced on&nbsp;their own, from&nbsp;conversations with&nbsp;clients, and&nbsp;from&nbsp;the&nbsp;fact that&nbsp;a&nbsp;single user persona could&nbsp;never be&nbsp;enough.</p>

      <p>All of it came out of conversations with clients. Companies were approaching us about automating work and bringing AI into their operations. But a fully custom solution was expensive at first, and AI itself was often hard to grasp as a topic. Somewhere in those conversations, it clicked: instead of building them complex systems right away, people first needed to learn the small, everyday habits, ideally on their own.</p>

      <p>We had the know-how, they had the need. We wanted to bridge that gap. When we suggested packaging our knowledge as an academy, they were sold.</p>

      <p>But when I sat down to define the users and tried to build a standard persona, I hit a wall. Fitting every prospective participant into one profile turned out to be impossible. Their starting lines were completely different: some had never opened AI, others used it daily but without any system.</p>

      <p>Splitting the offer into four levels, Foundation, Power, Advanced, and Expert, wasn't a marketing move. It came out of pure necessity. I wanted every user to recognize themselves in a description of their own situation right away, instead of wading through content meant for someone else.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cs-section cs-section-surface" id="test">
  <div class="cs-chapter-grid">
    <div class="cs-chapter-heading"><h2>The hero section carried a great atmosphere and zero&nbsp;context.</h2></div>
    <div class="cs-chapter-content">
      <p class="cs-subtitle">A&nbsp;test with&nbsp;five people had&nbsp;one job: find out if&nbsp;someone understands what&nbsp;the&nbsp;page offers within a&nbsp;few&nbsp;seconds.</p>

      <p>I decided to check first-impression clarity the fastest way that actually answers that question: a guerrilla comprehensibility test with five people. The goal wasn't a multi-week study, it was an immediate answer to one question: does someone understand, within the first five seconds, where they are and what the page offers?</p>

      <p>The result was unambiguous. Everyone tested stalled at the same point. The visual pulled them in, but they couldn't explain what it meant.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
