        </video>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cs-section cs-section-surface" id="reflection">
  <div class="cs-chapter-grid">
    <div class="cs-chapter-heading"><h2>I carried most of this page myself. The idea for the academy wasn't&nbsp;mine.</h2></div>
    <div class="cs-chapter-content">
      <p class="cs-subtitle">It&nbsp;came from&nbsp;the&nbsp;client, who&nbsp;saw a&nbsp;real product in&nbsp;that idea before&nbsp;anyone else&nbsp;did.</p>

      <p>Even though I owned most of the work on this page, from the content philosophy and visual design to the design system, the HTML prototype, and converting the frontend code into production Angular, the idea for the academy wasn't mine. My job was to give that vision a solid shape, while our engineering partner handled the backend.</p>

      <p>The biggest lesson from the whole project ran deeper than I expected: clarity of the message always has to come first. Atmosphere and experience come after. Emotion and aesthetics are meant to support the information, not overpower it. The hero section test proved exactly that: atmosphere alone simply wasn't enough.</p>

      <p>One part of the page stays deliberately unfinished: the business section. Today it's just a short paragraph for B2B leads, but I plan to turn it into a clear CTA with its own dedicated inquiry form.</p>

      <p>The landing page is live at <a href="https://puls.praut.cz" target="_blank" rel="noopener">puls.praut.cz</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
